      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Since the ellipse is tangent to the $x$-axis at $(4, 0)$ and tangent to the $y$-axis at $(0, 1),$ the equation of the ellipse can be written as:\n\\[\\frac{(x-4)^2}{16} + \\frac{(y-1)^2}{1} = 1.\\]\n\nThe distance between the foci of an ellipse is $2c,$ where $c$ is the distance from the center of the ellipse to each focus. The distance from the center to each focus is $c = \\sqrt{a^2 - b^2},$ where $a$ is the semi-major axis and $b$ is the semi-minor axis.\n\nIn our case, $a = 4$ and $b = 1.$ So, we have:\n\\[c = \\sqrt{4^2 - 1^2} = \\sqrt{15}.\\]\n\nThe distance between the foci is $2c,$ so the foci are located at $(4 \\pm \\sqrt{15}, 0)$ on the $x$-axis and $(0,1 \\pm \\sqrt{15})$ on the $y$-axis. The distance between the foci is:\n\\[2c = 2\\sqrt{15}.\\]\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{2\\sqrt{15}}.$",
