      "text": "We can draw a picture of what we are trying to figure out and label the relevant parts with symbols/proportionality constants.\n\nSince the axes of the ellipse are parallel to the coordinate axes and they are tangent to the axes it follows that they each have slope $\\pm 1$ as they pass through the origin.\n\nLet $\\ell_{1}$ be the line that passes through $(4,0)$ with slope $1$ and $\\ell_{2}$ be the line that passes through $(0,1)$ with slope $-1$ such that they are perpendicular.\n\nLet the points $P$ and $Q$ be the points where the tangents have slope $1$ and $-1$ respectively. These points of tangency must lie on the ellipse so we let the equations for $\\ell_{1}$ and $\\ell_{2}$ intersect the ellipse at points $P$ and $Q$ and we can let the slope of line $\\ell_{1}$ multipled by the y-axis is $(y-P)-(y_{P} - x_{P}) = 1$ and the slope of $\\ell_{2}$ is the same -- and this describes the consistent intersection/formula we get in terms of the poin $P$ -- whenever $\\ell_{1}$ and $\\ell_{2}$ both had slope $1$ and $-1$ respectively.\n\nAs $P$ and $Q$ are points of tangency, and they are similar the idea to slope is that if we have a straight line on the origin our tangent line -blue- must be symmetric, which the intersection points must be as well since $P$ can be swapped with $Q$ to form the other tangent line given the order of the tangent lines is switched.\n\nIn order to get the intersection of $\\ell_{1}$ and $\\ell_{2}$ in the equation of the ellipse them, we want in the form $x^{2}+y^{2}=a^{2}+b^{2}$ where $a$ and $b$ are the semi-major and semi-minor axes lengths respectively.\n\nThus by solving for $x,y$ we get $\\frac{x^{2}}{a^{2}}+\\frac{y^{2}}{b^{2}}=1$ and after we plug in $\\frac{y^{2}}{b^{2}}$ with $1-\\frac{x^{2}}{a^{2}}$ so $\\frac{x^{2}}{a^{2}}+\\frac{1-\\frac{x^{2}}{a^{2}}}{b^{2}}=1$ which results in $a^{2}+b^{2}=a^{2}b^{2}$ which means $a=\\sqrt[b]{b}$.\n\nSince the midpoints of $P,Q$ are $(2,0) and (0,1)$ and $P,Q$ are points of tangency which means that the line on the origin must be perpendicular to each other.\n\nFirst we introduce variables to represent the midpoint of tangent lines which are $P(s_{1},t_{1})$ and $Q(s_{2},t_{2})$. Note that the tangency points have coordinates $P(4, |b-\\sqrt{b^{2}}|)$ and $Q(-2, |b+\\sqrt{b^{2}}|)$ these are derived from the aforementioned they are on the line through the tangency point which must the lines through the center. Just note that $\\mid P\\mid$ and $\\mid Q\\mid$ are perpendicular since the lines opposite from each other since we want the ellipse to have foci using this ordering of variables $\\mid P\\mid$ must lie on a line with unique slope run/rend $\\frac{t_{1}-s_{1}}{b-\\sqrt{b^{2}}-s_{2}}=\\frac{t_{2}-s_{2}}{|b+\\sqrt{b^{2}}-s_{1}|}$ this reappears as $t_{1}=t_{2}$ so $\\frac{t_{1}+s_{1}}{2}=(s_{2}+t_{2})=\\frac{t_{1}+s_{2}}{2}$ becomes $\\frac{s_{1}+2s_{2}}{2}-t_{1}=b~ \\mid P\\not=Q\\mid$ and this gives the same $b$ and $r$ from last time which is unique.\n\nNow that we have uniquely defined the tangency points we can use the distance from the origin to our various quantities and perform some simplification.\n\nFirst we can define the distance from the origin to line $\\ell_{1}$ infinitesimally $d_{1}=\\frac{1}{\\left orient\\ right(\\overline{\\ell_{1}}\\left(\\frac{0-b}{b}\\right)+b\\frac{1}{r}\\right)}$ as follows.\n\n$r=|b|^{2}$ and $\\left orient\\right(\\overline{\\ell_{1}}\\left(\\frac{-b}{b}\\right)+b\\frac{1}{r})=1+\\frac{b^{2}}{rb}$\n\nand we can use similar steps for finding $d_{1}$ and $d_{2}$ meaning $\\mid d_{1}\\mid{\\le}d_{2}$ defines the circle of foci of our ellipse and $d_{2}=2$ must occur when the circle is tangent to the axes.\n\nI know of a way for finding the foci for an ellipse: The answer being that it is derived as follows. We let $a^{2}\\le b^{2}$ and denote $f$ as the y-coordinate of the foci. Define $\\overline{\\ell_{1}}(\\frac{b}{b})=-\\sqrt{b^{2}}+4$ and $\\overline{\\ell_{2}}(\\frac{b}{b})=-\\sqrt{b^{2}}-4$\n\nSo if $\\frac{\\overline{\\ell_{1}}\\left(\\frac{b}{b}\\right)}{\\left orient\\right(-\\overline{\\ell_{2}}\\left(\\frac{b}{b}\\right))}$ of sorts our point on the tangent at origin is $(b,f)$ and it follows Insy tanca that the numerator of $\\frac{1}{2a+b}$ inside of the square root is $f^{2}-b^{2}$ so we have $f=\\sqrt{b^{2}-\\frac{d_{2}^{2}}{4}}$. This means that our semi-minor ax axis length is $b^{2}$ decreases by $\\pm \\sqrt{b^{2}-\\frac{d_{2}^{2}}{4}}$ which we end up with plus and minus the from of the foci drawn but with a radius of $\\frac{d_{2}}{2}$.\n\nTherefore $2a+b=\\sqrt{b^{2}-\\frac{d_{2}^{2}}{4}}$. This means that the distance between the foci must be $d_{2}-a=\\sqrt{b^{2}-\\frac{d_{2}^{2}}{4}}-a$ which then simplifies to find $a^{2}-a-1$",
